  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PARTNERSHIP FOR THE CONSERVATION OF CENTRAL AMERICA&apos;S 5 GREAT FORESTS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THIS PROJECT BUILDS UPON PREVIOUS WORK CONDUCTED UNDER A FIVE-YEAR COOPERATIVE AGREEMENT THAT ESTABLISHED A BROAD ALLIANCE AND FIVE-PILLAR STRATEGY TO CONSERVE CENTRAL AMERICAS FIVE GREAT FORESTS. THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO IMPLEMENT THREE PROJECTS IN THREE DIFFERENT COUNTRIES, REPRESENTING COMPLEMENTARY, DISCRETE, AND TANGIBLE EXAMPLES OF THE FIVE GREAT FORESTS STRATEGY IN ACTION AT THE LOCAL LEVEL. CENTRAL AMERICAS WILDLIFE AND WILD PLACES, AS WELL AS THE PHYSICAL, CULTURAL, AND FOOD AND WATER SECURITY OF INDIGENOUS PEOPLES AND LOCAL COMMUNITIES, ARE AT RISK FROM ENVIRONMENTAL DEGRADATION, CLIMATE CHANGE, AND THE EXPANSION OF ILLEGAL CATTLE RANCHING. THE PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE THREE AREAS WITHIN THE FIVE GREAT FORESTS, INCLUDING VIABLE POPULATIONS OF WIDE-RANGING SPECIES, SUCH AS THE JAGUAR, BAIRDS TAPIR, WHITE-LIPPED PECCARY, MACAW, AND CRITICALLY ENDANGERED CENTRAL AMERICAN RIVER TURTLE, AS WELL AS SUPPORT THE FOREST COMMUNITIES THAT RELY ON THEM. ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) STATE-OF-THE-ART FOREST PROTECTION IN THE MAYA FOREST CORRIDOR OF BELIZE 2) RESTORATION OF DEGRADED CATTLE LANDSCAPES TO GENERATE ECONOMIC AND ENVIRONMENTAL BENEFITS IN CARMELITA COMMUNITY FOREST CONCESSION, MAYA BIOSPHERE RESERVE, GUATEMALA AND 3) INDIGENOUS-LED FOREST PROTECTION AND FOREST-BASED LOCAL ECONOMIES IN BOSAWAS BIOSPHERE RESERVE, NICARAGUA</narrative>
  </description>
